      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 S. Karger AG, Basel.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Iodopovidone is an alternative agent used to promote pleurodesis in patients with malignant pleural effusion (MPE). However, safety is a concern, and many authors still reject its use."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Our main objective is to describe the occurrence of common and severe adverse events after pleurodesis with two different doses of iodopovidone in patients with MPE. Our secondary objective is to evaluate dose dependency, efficacy, quality of life, and systemic inflammation."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "We conducted a double-blind, randomized clinical trial including patients with recurrent MPE. Patients underwent chest tube insertion and were randomized into two groups according to the doses of iodopovidone: group 1 received 1% iodopovidone, and group 2 received 2% iodopovidone. During follow-up, adverse events, inflammatory markers, quality of life, and imaging exams were systematically evaluated and registered."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Sixty patients were analyzed (55 females, 5 males, median age 55.9 years). Overall, 227 adverse events possibly related to pleurodesis were registered, including 47 serious adverse events (in 34 patients). Pleuritic pain and hypertensive peaks were the most frequently observed serious adverse events (11 and 10 episodes, respectively). Grade 3/4 metabolic events such as hyponatremia and an increase in alkaline phosphatase, AST and ALT levels were also common. C-reactive protein (CRP) levels increased substantially and peaked 48 h after pleurodesis. No difference was observed between groups with regard to adverse events, CRP levels, efficacy, or quality of life."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Adverse events after iodopovidone pleurodesis in patients with MPE are common and similar in the two doses studied."
          }
        ]
      },
